            <div xml:id="d387">

                <pb facs="0478" n="398" sameAs="frus:frus1930v01/pg398"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">863.51 Relief Credits/221: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dawes</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-05-02T11:00:00+01:00">May 2, 1930—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 2—6 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>88. Department’s 93, April 15, 6 p.m. In letter dated May 1, 1930, Foreign Office
                    refers to the letter, dated April 16, 1930, sent <pb facs="0479" n="399"
                        xml:id="pg399"/> by Sir Frederick Leith-Ross to the Austrian Minister (text
                    of which was contained in Embassy’s 69, April 17, 3 P.M.), and states: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Sir Frederick Leith-Ross, besides being Chairman of the International
                            Relief Bonds Committee, is of course the representative of His Majesty’s
                            Government on the Committee and the letter which he wrote was written
                            with their authority and embodies their intention.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
